337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 130 0 R
/T (Blister gum packaging of the redesign is classified as PET contact-sensitive packaging. This means that by 2030, it must be made of at least 30% recyclable material. By 2040, this increases to a minimum of 50%. )
/E (Blister gum packaging of the redesign is classified as PET contact-sensitive packaging. This means that by 2030, it must be made of at least 30% recyclable material. By 2040, this increases to a minimum of 50%. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (We intend to use a minimum of 50% rPET film. However, flexible films with 30-50% food-grade rPET are not widely available. If demand cannot be met, a PET film with a lower amount of recycled material than deemed necessary by the PPWR could be used. As we are working with food packaging, using 30-50% rPET film is only a legal requirement if it can be safely done. If the amount of EFSA-compliant food-grade rPET is less than needed or the used recycling processes do not exist, an exemption can be made. For this, it is necessary to prove that PET film is the only suitable material for the product, or that using another material will deteriorate either the product or its recyclability or sustainability targets.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 130 0 R
/T (Packaging can only qualify as "reusable" under strict criteria; it must have and survive multiple use cycles and there must be systems for reuse in place, not a possibility to. )
/E (Packaging can only qualify as "reusable" under strict criteria; it must have and survive multiple use cycles and there must be systems for reuse in place, not a possibility to. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (The \(r\)PET sleeve can survive multiple reuse cycles and will be cleaned and restored in between them. The system for reuse is fully worked out. After the blister has run out, the consumer will have to carry the empty sleeve with them until they go to the store again, as a return on-the-go system is used. With this, we aim for a sequential reuse system, where the company is responsible for cleaning and restoring the secondary packaging. For ease of access, a collaboration with Albert Heijn is used, where return bins will be places in the store, next to the exit. This way, consumers do not need to alter their lifestyles to properly dispose of the product. This reuse system will be tested on a smaller scale beforehand.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
